From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CBOC-Vita <344212538+CBOC-Vita@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:53:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 72/73] gl: gfx.antialiasing=auto, the default for new players: FXAA
 while the GPU has room, off while it has not

Antialiasing gets a fifth value, Auto, first in the list and the default of the Vita app. The pass is the cheaper FXAA
(gl_fxaa_quality=fast); Auto turns it off when the renderer falls behind and back on when it has room again, so a casual player
gets the smoother edges where they fit and a steady frame rate where they do not. Off, FXAA, MSAA 2x and MSAA 4x are what they were.

host/gpu/auto_aa.hpp is a plain state machine, one sample per presented frame, no GL and no clock of its own:
* late frame: the interval since the last present is over 125 % of the 30 Hz budget (41.7 ms) and the renderer side is to blame
  (the present waited 6 ms or more for the GPU, the split GL thread was 12 ms or more behind the walker, or a frame was skipped);
  a frame that was late because the game's own code was slow is not FXAA's doing;
* off after 2 late 3D frames in a row, at once on a skipped frame; menus, movies and loads change nothing, and an interval over
  200 ms is a pause, not a frame time;
* back on after 10 s with no late frame, at least 150 3D frames and a GL thread that slept 3 ms or more per frame on average;
* a try that fails within 6 s doubles the wait (up to 80 s) and asks for 1.5 times the room (up to 9 ms); a try that holds for a
  minute is forgiven; the first 2 frames after it comes on are not counted.
The renderer reads the GL thread's lag, its idle time (the GlIdle phase timer) and the skipped-frame count through a new
Renderer::set_load_probe that the split renderer fills in.

The log has "[aa] auto: fxaa off/on (...) [change N]" for every change and "[gl] speed features: ..." once at start. Options:
gl_aa_auto=0 is the old behaviour (Auto behaves as Off), =2 forces FXAA on; gl_aa_auto_retry_s, gl_aa_auto_need_idle_us,
gl_aa_auto_budget_ms and gl_aa_auto_min_frames set the thresholds; gl_test_slow_* makes a frame slow on purpose for a test.
Auto never adds FXAA to a run that started with MSAA (MSAA needs a restart and is only ever chosen by the player). The menu row
reads "Auto (on)" or "Auto (off)" once something is known.

Settings saved by 1.0 wrote every key, so a choice cannot be told from a default: Settings::adopt_auto_antialiasing() moves a file
to Auto only when its anti-aliasing is Off and every other row is at its default (a player who changed nothing else); a file with
other changes keeps its Off. port::apply_vita_defaults() is the Vita app's platform defaults in one place; ge_replay --menu-as-vita
uses it. The FXAA pass is pre-linked at start-up when Auto may use it.

tests/auto_aa_tests.cpp drives the state machine with synthetic frame timings (steady 30 fps stays on; two late frames switch
off; a skipped frame at once; a slow game thread is not blamed; menus and pauses are ignored; the retry waits, back-off and
forgiveness; change counts). tests/port_settings_tests.cpp covers the new value, its labels and the migration cases.

Checked with software GL on mission-1 replays: Auto at full speed is the same picture as FXAA frame for frame, gl_aa_auto=0 the same
as Off, and with a slow frame injected the pass goes off and comes back after the quiet time. Not measured on a Vita: the
thresholds are a first estimate and have not been tuned on a console.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
